To: oss-security@lists.openwall.com

Point is, many "security researchers" overly pretend to work for security=20
hiding intention for fame / paper publications / product selling.

Accept unnecessary long embargoes then you support this behaviour but=20
might provide patches faster after public release.
Reject unnecessary long embargoes then you discourage this behaviour but=20
might provide patches slower after public release next time.

I think the decision is always individual per vulnerability weighting how=20
grave/easy to find/abuse it is vs. how interested you are in future early=20
reports.
Keeping it individual without public announced maximum embargo time would=20
also help prevent folks from jumping to 0daying everything per default:)

